# Solution

We determine all sequences $\langle a_1, a_2, \ldots \rangle$ of positive integers such that $\gcd(a_i, a_j) = \gcd(i, j)$ for all distinct $i, j$.

**Claim.** The unique solution is $a_n = n$ for all $n \geq 1$.

## Proof

**Step 1: Reduction to prime valuations.**

For a prime $p$, let $v_p(m)$ denote the $p$-adic valuation of $m$. Since
$$\gcd(x, y) = \prod_p p^{\min(v_p(x),\, v_p(y))},$$
the condition $\gcd(a_i, a_j) = \gcd(i, j)$ for all $i \neq j$ is equivalent to:

> For every prime $p$ and all $i \neq j$: $\min\!\big(v_p(a_i),\, v_p(a_j)\big) = \min\!\big(v_p(i),\, v_p(j)\big).$

It suffices to show that for each prime $p$, this forces $v_p(a_n) = v_p(n)$ for every $n$.

**Step 2: Encoding via level sets.**

Fix a prime $p$ and write $c_n := v_p(n)$, $b_n := v_p(a_n)$. For each integer $k \geq 1$, define the level sets
$$T_k := \{n \geq 1 : b_n \geq k\}, \qquad S_k := \{n \geq 1 : c_n \geq k\} = \{n : p^k \mid n\} = p^k\,\mathbb{N}.$$

The key observation is:
$$\min(b_i, b_j) \geq k \iff b_i \geq k \text{ and } b_j \geq k \iff i, j \in T_k,$$
and similarly $\min(c_i, c_j) \geq k \iff i, j \in S_k$.

Since $\min(b_i, b_j) = \min(c_i, c_j)$ for all $i \neq j$, we obtain:

> For every $k \geq 1$: $\{(i,j) : i \neq j,\; i,j \in T_k\} = \{(i,j) : i \neq j,\; i,j \in S_k\}.$

**Step 3: The level sets are determined.**

Each $S_k = p^k\,\mathbb{N}$ is **infinite** (it contains $p^k, 2p^k, 3p^k, \ldots$), so $|S_k| \geq 2$.

For any set $U$ with $|U| \geq 2$, the off-diagonal pair set $\{(i,j) : i \neq j,\; i,j \in U\}$ determines $U$ completely: an element $n$ belongs to $U$ if and only if there exists $m \in U$ with $m \neq n$ and $(n, m)$ appears in the pair set.

Since $|S_k| \geq 2$, the equality of off-diagonal pair sets forces $T_k = S_k$ for every $k \geq 1$.

**Step 4: Conclusion for a single prime.**

We have shown $T_k = S_k$ for all $k \geq 1$, i.e.,
$$b_n \geq k \iff p^k \mid n \quad \text{for all } k \geq 1.$$
This is precisely the definition of $b_n = v_p(n)$. Hence $v_p(a_n) = v_p(n)$ for all $n$.

**Step 5: Combining all primes.**

Step 4 holds for **every** prime $p$. Therefore $v_p(a_n) = v_p(n)$ for all primes $p$ and all $n \geq 1$. Since a positive integer is determined by its valuations at all primes,
$$a_n = \prod_p p^{v_p(a_n)} = \prod_p p^{v_p(n)} = n \quad \text{for all } n \geq 1.$$

**Verification.** For $a_n = n$, we have $\gcd(a_i, a_j) = \gcd(i, j)$ trivially. ∎

The unique sequence satisfying the condition is $\boxed{a_n = n \text{ for all } n \geq 1}$.

